### JavaScript Function: `processStringWithConstraints`

```javascript
function processStringWithConstraints(inputStr, sizeLimit, maxMemoryThreshold, domTree, selector, callback) {
    if (!inputStr || !domTree || !selector || typeof callback !== 'function') return null;

    // Check memory constraints for the string operation
    let potentialSize = inputStr.length + 1;
    if (potentialSize > sizeLimit || (globalThis.memoryUsage().heapUsed / 1024) > maxMemoryThreshold) {
        return null;
    }

    const modifiedString = `${inputStr}-`;

    // Recursive function to traverse the DOM tree
    function traverseDOM(node, result = []) {
        if (!node) return result;

        if (callback(node)) {
            result.push(node);
        }

        for (let child of node.children || []) {
            traverseDOM(child, result);
        }

        return result;
    }

    const matchingElements = traverseDOM(domTree).filter(el => el.matches(selector));

    return [modifiedString, matchingElements];
}
```

These implementations handle the specified constraints and ensure efficient memory management. The Python function uses subprocess to execute `sed` commands for text replacement, while the C function manages a linked list without exceeding memory limits. The JavaScript function processes strings under memory constraints and efficiently traverses large DOM trees using recursion.']

